/-ai
The binary join map sends elements x and y to the binary finite join of x and y. The binary join map from the product of an effectively given domain to its power domain is computable: its neighbourhood relation is two independent singleton tests. The binary join relates X and Y to W if and only if both singleton maps relate their argument to W. The binary finite join of x and y contains a power-domain neighbourhood W if and only if both singleton joins do. The binary finite join of x and y equals the meet of the singleton of x and the singleton of y. The singleton map relates the enumerated neighbourhood of n to the neighbourhood coded by k if and only if some index in the decoded list of k lies above the neighbourhood of n. The singleton map sends each element x to the singleton of x. If a neighbourhood X' is contained in a neighbourhood X, then the singleton of the principal element of X lies below the singleton of the principal element of X'. The singleton of the principal element of a neighbourhood X equals the principal element of the power domain generated by the down-set of X. When a domain is effectively given, the singleton map is a computable map from its presentation to the induced presentation of the power domain. The flat two-point domain is not a subdomain of its power domain.

The numbered name theorem_712 is the conjunction of these statements. The proof is that conjunction.
ai-/
